# Security standards (auto-loaded)

Source of truth: `../THE-NEXUS/04-STANDARDS/security-standards.md` and `../THE-NEXUS/04-STANDARDS/secrets-handling.md`.

## Hard rules

### Always

- Encrypt at rest. Encrypt in transit. No exceptions for "internal" services.
- Default-deny network. Justify every public surface.
- Least-privilege IAM. No `*` actions/resources without an inline comment justifying.
- Pinned, lockfiled, scanned dependencies.
- Logs sanitized: no secrets, no PII, no auth tokens.
- Audit trails on Confidential/Restricted data access.

### Ask first (before producing the change)

- New public-facing endpoint.
- New external dependency.
- Sending Confidential data to any third-party SaaS (including AI).
- Lowering an IAM scope to "make things work".

### Never

- Commit secrets. Pre-commit `gitleaks` runs.
- Log secrets, PHI, tokens, session keys.
- HTTP for non-public surfaces.
- Roll your own crypto.
- Default credentials anywhere.
- Send Restricted data to a cloud AI provider.

## When auth/secrets/IAM/network is touched

Hand to security-auditor before "done". See `../THE-NEXUS/02-PERSONAS/security-auditor.md`.

## STRIDE-lite checklist

Apply to any feature touching auth, data, or external surfaces:

- Spoofing? Tampering? Repudiation? Info disclosure? DoS amplification? Elevation?
